<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit Bahiani</h1><p class="st-meta">950 BCE – 808 BCE (approx.) · capital Guzana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bit Bahiani, an Aramaean kingdom of the upper Khabur centred on Guzana, famous for the sculpted palace of King Kapara at Tell Halaf.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aramaean tribes took over the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> in the 10th century BCE, and the House of Bahiani ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Guzana</a>, at Tell Halaf on the Syrian-Turkish border. In 894 BCE its king Abi-salamu paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-ii">Adad-nirari II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapara">Kapara</a> built a palace whose facade had colossal statues of gods on animals and dozens of carved stone reliefs. Excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-von-oppenheim">Max von Oppenheim</a> from 1911, they were shattered by bombing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1943 and painstakingly restored. A bilingual statue of the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad-yithi">Hadad-yith'i</a> in Assyrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> shows the kingdom adapting to Assyrian rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bit Bahiani became an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and finally a province c. 808 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Guzana</a> is the Gozan of the Bible, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> were deported after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bit-bahiani.jpg" alt="Bit Bahiani" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Kmhkmh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aramaic_kingdoms_and_chiefdoms_around_10-9th_century_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 BCE – 808 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guzana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abi-salamu, Kapara, Hadad-yith'i</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Aramaean religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guzana, Gozan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-879&amp;focus=bit-bahiani" aria-label="Bit Bahiani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5685 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bit Bahiani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6005 1106L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6005 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6005 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-879&amp;focus=bit-bahiani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

